## Snapshots & the git model

- **DB snapshot before every Tier 1–3 migration** → instant undo through the existing
  `git_restore` path. Tier 0 needs none. Snapshots use `pg_dump` *inside the container*
  (no host Postgres required):

  ```bash
  docker compose exec -T db pg_dump -U postgres payload   > .mvp/snapshots/<sha>.sql
  docker compose exec -T db psql    -U postgres -d payload < .mvp/snapshots/<sha>.sql
  ```

- **Snapshot-per-commit** (keyed by SHA, in a gitignored `.mvp/snapshots/` or app-data
  dir) ties DB state to git saves. "Restore to save X" = `git read-tree X` (already
  done) **+ load snapshot X**. This is what makes **restore and branch switching**
  DB-consistent despite Payload having no down-to-target — and it pairs with tombstones:
  even after GC, restoring an older snapshot brings the data back.

